mode: build

--- OUT --->
6
--- UNIT_IR --->
# **per unit and with no merge**, which is what makes this different from every other IR section in the
# corpus: `--print-unit-ir` prints each compilation unit as the object writer receives it, where `-p` prints
# the one module `-O` folded them into. On a `mode: build` case those are genuinely different programs.

CHECK: [unit main]

# `nonnull` is the type system's own claim about a borrow, finally said out loud - `T&` differs from
# `ptr<T>` by one bit on ValueType and both used to lower to a bare `ptr`. `nocapture` and `readonly` are
# LLVM's, derived from a body it can now reason about
CHECK: define i32 @_totalZZMRBCLC5arrayIMLPiE(ptr nocapture nonnull readonly

# the length is read once, in the entry block, rather than through `count()` per turn - which is what gives
# the loop a computable trip count, and with it unrolling and vectorisation
CHECK: entry:
CHECK: load i64

# **the loop vectorises on this path too**, which is the part that makes it worth asserting here as well as
# in iteration/lowered_cost: this is the *per unit* O2 pipeline rather than the whole-program O3 one, so it
# pins that an ordinary `echoc build` gets the same treatment a `-O` build does. A widened loop is also the
# strongest available statement that `count()` and the element operator were inlined away and the bounds
# `assert` was dropped - a loop with a call left in it cannot be vectorised at all.
#
# spelled `vector.body:` rather than `body:` deliberately; iteration/lowered_cost carries why
CHECK: vector.body:
CHECK-NOT: call
CHECK-NOT: alloca
CHECK: load <4 x i32>
CHECK: add <4 x i32>

# and the scalar tail is the indexed load and the loop's own compare, with nothing else. the
# `llvm.vector.reduce.add` folding the accumulators is in `middle.block` between the two, outside either
# loop, so neither CHECK-NOT region covers it
CHECK: getelementptr i32
CHECK-NOT: call
CHECK-NOT: alloca
CHECK: br i1
